  <section>
    <h2>How the donation route works</h2>
    <p>Telegram has a built-in gift function: you can send Stars from your balance to another account. That function is what makes this possible — nothing unusual, nothing hidden.</p>
    <ol>
      <li>Message support at <a href="https://telegram.me/starsjoy_bot" target="_blank" rel="noopener">@starsjoy_bot</a> and say how many Stars you want to convert.</li>
      <li>Agree the current rate and the total in som. Confirm it before sending anything — the rate is quoted at that moment, not calculated afterwards.</li>
      <li>Send the Stars as a gift to the account you are given.</li>
      <li>Receive the transfer to your Uzcard or Humo card.</li>
    </ol>
    <p>At no point does anyone need your password, your SMS login code or your 2FA password. If a service asks for those, it is not processing a payout — it is taking your account.</p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Expect the rate to be lower than what you paid</h2>
    <p>This is the part that surprises people, so it is worth being blunt about it. Buying Stars and selling them back are not the same price, and they never will be. The gap covers Telegram's own commission on the transfer plus the cost of the payout.</p>
    <p>Any service quoting you the same rate in both directions is either mistaken or planning not to pay. Ask for the number in the bot before you send Stars, and treat a refusal to quote as a warning sign.</p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>When cashing out is the wrong move</h2>
    <p>If you are holding Stars you intend to spend inside Telegram anyway, converting them is a loss. Stars do not expire and do not lose value sitting in your balance, so there is no pressure to move them. Cashing out makes sense when you received Stars you have no use for — as a gift, or from a channel that pays in Stars — not as a way to manage a balance you will spend eventually.</p>
    <p>If you are a creator earning meaningful volume, the official Fragment route is worth setting up despite the friction: it scales, and the 21-day wait only bites once.</p>
  </section>

